def is_nested(string):
    opening = []
    closing = []
    for i, c in enumerate(string):
        if c == '[':
            opening.append(i)
        elif c == ']':
            closing.append(i)
    closing.reverse()
    count = 0
    for i, idx in enumerate(opening):
        if i < len(closing) and idx < closing[i]:
            count += 1
    return count >= 2
```

This pairs the first opening bracket with the last closing bracket, the second opening with the second-to-last closing, and so on. It returns True when at least two such pairs are in the right order, because then one pair sits inside another. I checked it by hand against two of the examples and didn't run it. I also compared it with the usual reference approach, which only advances the closing index after a successful match. Once a pair fails, every later pair fails too, so the two give the same result.

